    "https://listings.jobs.gmu.edu/jobs/tenure-track-assistant-professor-of-communication-specialty-in-new-emerging-communication-technology-and-innovative-research-methods-fairfax-va-virginia-united-states-other": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Communication (specialty in New/Emerging Communication Technology and Innovative Research Methods)",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Communication (specialty in New/Emerging Communication Technology and Innovative Research Methods) - Fairfax, VA, Virginia, United States Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Communication (specialty in New/Emerging Communication Technology and Innovative Research Methods) - Fairfax, VA, Virginia, United States Kiosk mode Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Communication (specialty in New/Emerging Communication Technology and Innovative Research Methods) Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Communication (specialty in New/Emerging Communication Technology and Innovative Research Methods) 10004638 Fairfax, VA Instructional Faculty Opening on: Oct 9 2026 Department: Col of Humanities and Soc Science Classification: 9-month Instructional Faculty Job Category: Instructional Faculty Job Type: Full-Time Work Schedule: Full-time (1.0 FTE, 40 hrs/wk) Location: Fairfax, VA Workplace Type: On Site Required Sponsorship Eligibility: Eligible for visa sponsorship Salary: Salary commensurate with education and experience Criminal Background Check: Yes About the Department: Mason’s Communication Department has a diverse and collegial faculty committed to excellence, innovation, community and public service, inclusion, and social equity. All department faculty participate in the life of our campus community, including career fairs, faculty functions, recruiting sessions and other events. Located just 15 miles from Washington, D.C., we offer a broad undergraduate program with more than 300 majors; six minor programs; a vibrant MA program including strategic communication; health communication; science, environment, and risk communication; and media and culture specialties; and a highly ranked PhD program emphasizing health; strategic; and science, environment, and risk communication. The department also offers a graduate certificate in science communication. We serve a diverse population of undergraduate and graduate students who bring a wide variety of cultures and backgrounds to our classrooms. Additionally, the department supports several co-curricular activities, including debate, forensics, Public Relations Student Society of America (PRSSA), Society of Professional Journalists (SPJ), and a comprehensive student media program. The research life of the department includes both undergraduate and graduate research and is reflected in the activities and outreach of its faculty, including those of the Center for Climate Change Communication (4C); the Communication, Health, and Relational Media (CHARM) research lab; the Center for Health and Risk Communication (CHRC); the Pop Culture lab; and the Character Assassination and Reputation Politics (CARP) research lab, in addition to several university-wide research centers and institutes including the Institute for Digital Innovation. For more information on the Department of Communication at George Mason University, visit communication.gmu.edu . About the Position: The Department of Communication in the College of Humanities and Social Sciences at George Mason University seeks a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Communication, with a specialty in new/emerging communication technology and innovative research methods. Applicants should have a PhD in Communication or a closely related field. We welcome candidates from any communication subfield. We are especially interested in those whose work engages one or more of our core areas: strategic communication and public relations; health communication; science, environment, and risk communication; media studies; journalism; or advocacy and political communication. The successful candidate will demonstrate an active research agenda focused on new/emerging communication technologies with innovative research methods, a record of competitive conference papers and/or scholarly publications commensurate with experience, an ongoing research agenda, a commitment to excellence in teaching and mentoring a diverse student body, and a willingness to contribute to departmental and university service. We welcome all methodological approaches and specifically seek a candidate who pursues innovative methods to examine and/or utilize new and emerging technology. Candidates should demonstrate an ability to teach graduate and undergraduate courses in a variety of areas, as listed above. Overall, teaching experience at the university level is preferred. Finally, an interest in developing curriculum and courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels is also preferred. Responsibilities: Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in communication, including courses related to new and emerging technologies; Mentor and advise students; Develop and maintain an active research agenda focused on new and emerging communication technologies using innovative research methods; and Contribute to departmental, college, university, and professional service through committee participation, faculty governance, program and curriculum activities, and service to the discipline. Required Qualifications: PhD in communication, or related field, completed prior to appointment; Active research agenda grounded in the study of new/emergent media, with a record of competitive conference papers and/or published research commensurate with experience; Demonstration of quantitative or qualitative innovative research methods, such as advanced natural language processing (NLP) and text mining (AI content analysis), digital ethnography, online social network analysis, AI content generation, social media analytics and big data analysis, agent-based modeling, or other innovative technological approaches to research; Ability to teach courses at the undergraduate and graduate levels that contribute to our subareas as listed above; Ability to teach theory courses in new media and technology and within the candidate’s particular subfield of communication; Commitment to teaching and mentoring a diverse student community; and Commitment to service for the department, university, and discipline. Preferred Qualifications: PhD in communication (rather than a PhD in a related field); A record of relevant published research commensurate with experience; Relevant teaching experience at the college or university level; Experience with or interest in developing courses and curriculum at undergraduate and graduate levels; and Experience with or interest in undergraduate student mentoring and graduate student advising. Instructions to Applicants: In a continuing effort to enrich GMU’s academic environment and provide equal educational and employment opportunities, we actively encourage applications from all job seekers. For full consideration, applicants must apply at https://jobs.gmu.edu/ ; complete and submit the online application; and upload a CV, a cover letter describing their qualifications for the position, and a list of three professional references with contact information. For full consideration, please submit the above materials to GMU’s jobs portal ( https://jobs.gmu.edu/ ) by November 30, 2026. The jobs portal will remain open until the position is filled. Posting Open Date: October 9, 2026 For Full Consideration, Apply by: November 30, 2026 Open Until Filled: Yes Mason Ad Statement George Mason University is a nationally ranked R1 research university committed to creating a more just, free, and prosperous world. With 40,000 enrolled students, George Mason is the largest and most diverse public research university in Virginia, offering degree programs at the master's, doctoral, and professional level, along with certificates and credentials. George Mason fosters an All Together Different environment for students, faculty, and staff, driven by our core beliefs. We believe in inclusivity over exclusivity; we believe in advancing our mission by being willing to take risks, not avoiding them; and we believe our best work is possible when we apply our diversity of origin, identity, circumstance, and thought. Equity Statement George Mason University is an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, committed to promoting inclusion and equity in its community. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, disability or veteran status, or any characteristic protected by law. Campus Safety Information Mason’s Annual Security and Fire Safety Report is available at http://police.gmu.edu/annual-security-report/ Apply Now! Sign up for job alerts! Job Alert cfd0953d Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments Col of Humanities and Soc Science Col of Humanities and Soc Science Locations Fairfax, VA, Virginia, United States Fairfax, VA, Virginia, United States First Name (required) e167235f Last Name (required) 14630b17 Email (required) 6231a615 Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Similar Jobs Instructional Technologist Division of Enrollment Management Join George Mason University as an Instructional Technologist and help advance engaging, innovative online learning through instructional design, educational technology, and faculty support. Graduate Admissions Specialist & Academic Advisor Antonin Scalia Law School The Graduate Admissions Specialist & Academic Advisor at George Mason University’s Antonin Scalia Law School has administrative support from various departments within the law school and the university. This position is primarily responsible for supporting student facing office functions including meeting with prospective students, overseeing front desk and staff support, managing office supplies and supporting application management, supporting onboarding functions for admitted students, and guiding