      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Mission Software Engineer",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Mission Software Engineer - Fairfax, VA, Virginia, United States Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Mission Software Engineer - Fairfax, VA, Virginia, United States Kiosk mode Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Mission Software Engineer Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Mission Software Engineer 10004632 Fairfax, VA Postdoctoral Faculty Opening on: Oct 7 2026 Department: College of Science Classification: Post Doc 12 month Job Category: Postdoctoral Faculty Job Type: Full-Time Work Schedule: Full-time (1.0 FTE, 40 hrs/wk) Location: Fairfax, VA Workplace Type: Hybrid Eligible Sponsorship Eligibility: Not eligible for visa sponsorship Salary: Salary commensurate with education and experience Restricted: Yes Criminal Background Check: Yes About the Department: George Mason University (GMU), a Carnegie Research I institution, is Virginia’s largest and most diverse university with more than 38,000 students. Located in Fairfax, Virginia, Mason is part of the Washington DC metropolitan area offering easy access to the scientific and political resources of the nation’s capital. George Mason University is committed to advancing inclusion, access, and equal opportunity throughout our community. We believe that a broad range of voices, cultures, and perspectives brings vibrancy, innovation, and growth to an academic community. The Landolt Mission team is responsible for developing and executing a NASA space-based mission through coordinated work across science, payload, spacecraft bus, software, systems engineering, integration and test, mission assurance, and project management functions. The unit provides an interdisciplinary research and training environment for faculty, postdoctoral researchers, engineers, staff, and students working collaboratively with federal, academic, commercial, and other external partners. The Landolt Mission team supports mission formulation, systems engineering, subsystem coordination, technical trade studies, requirements development, verification and validation planning, assembly, integration and testing, technical documentation, mission reviews, and mission operations planning. The unit works collaboratively across payload, spacecraft bus, software, ground systems, mission operations, science, and programmatic interfaces to advance mission objectives and meet NASA requirements. Statement on Building an All-Inclusive Culture: George Mason University College of Science (Mason Science) is committed to advancing inclusive excellence and fostering an environment free from discrimination, harassment, and retaliation throughout our STEM community. At Mason Science, our values include cultivating an organizational culture that promotes belonging, respect, and civility. We believe that varied opinions, cultures, and perspectives are what provides vibrancy, innovation, and growth to an academic community. By prioritizing cultural responsiveness in academics, teaching, research, and global engagement, we strive to attract faculty and staff who exemplify the Mason Science mission and vision. About the Position: This position is sponsored by the NASA Landolt mission. The Mission Software Engineer (SE) duties include (at a different level of involvement and responsibility): Contribute to all aspects of mission software design and development, including creating, reviewing, testing, verifying, documenting, and integrating code as part of the mission software team.; Participate in documentation, integration, and testing of elements of mission software alongside personnel from other mission teams, including the payload team, the bus hardware team, and systems engineering team.; Perform data collection, analysis, and simulation studies as needed to predict, verify, and analyze functions of the mission software. Communicate key outcomes and findings to other members and leadership of the Landolt Mission; Document activities for the Landolt Mission through mission reviews, mission reports, mission documentation, and technical publications in a manner consistent with space-based projects and published results in professional scientific journals; Work collaboratively and effectively in a team environment composed of scientists, engineers, students, project management and support staff at multiple institutions, and federal, academic and commercial partners; and Foster a culture of engagement, diversity, inclusiveness, excellence and innovation. Champion GMU’s commitment to Access, Justice, Equity, Diversity, and Inclusion to create an inclusive and welcoming environment that promotes a commitment to safety, integrity, and teamwork. Responsibilities: Mission Software Development Generate, debug, and integrate code leading to the delivery of a flight-ready mission software package; Assist in software testing, verification, and integration; and Comply with NASA space systems requirements and procedures. Reporting and Coordinating Assist in the writing and production of formal technical reports, progress reports, and reviews for NASA (e.g. PDR, CDR, TRR, FRR, ORR) in a manner consistent with NASA’s documentation requirements, standards and guidelines (e.g. NPR 7120.5/8); Write or co-write papers for professional journals and conferences; Attend meetings with sponsors and collaborators, federal, commercial and academic partners, when necessary, occasionally requiring travel outside of the DC metropolitan area; and Assist in the pursuit of new and continued GMU Space Center projects and funding support (e.g. new, extended, and follow-on missions/projects). Required Qualifications: Terminal degree in Computer/Software Engineering, Aerospace Engineering, Systems Engineering, or related field with emphasis on space systems development; Experience producing working code in a relevant language (C, Python, JavaScript, etc.).; and Familiarity with industry best practices in software development. Preferred Qualifications: Experience with hybrid missions, operations planning, space hardware, rad-hard electronics, embedded system development, and/or NASA procedures and practices; Experience in practical software design, development, and testing including hardware systems or embedded systems is desirable; Familiarity with software development process in space mission context, including knowledge of or experience with NASA procedures and practices (or procedures and practices for other spacefaring organizations); and Knowledge of space environment, spacecraft subsystems, or optical payloads - especially laser systems. Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applicants must apply for Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Mission Software Engineer at https://jobs.gmu.edu/. Complete and submit the online application to include three professional references with contact information, and provide a CV with a Statement of Interest and Experience for review. Posting Open Date: October 7, 2026 For Full Consideration, Apply by: November 1, 2026 Open Until Filled: Yes Mason Ad Statement George Mason University is a nationally ranked R1 research university committed to creating a more just, free, and prosperous world. With 40,000 enrolled students, George Mason is the largest and most diverse public research university in Virginia, offering degree programs at the master's, doctoral, and professional level, along with certificates and credentials. George Mason fosters an All Together Different environment for students, faculty, and staff, driven by our core beliefs. We believe in inclusivity over exclusivity; we believe in advancing our mission by being willing to take risks, not avoiding them; and we believe our best work is possible when we apply our diversity of origin, identity, circumstance, and thought. Equity Statement George Mason University is an equal opportunity/affirmative action employer, committed to promoting inclusion and equity in its community. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, disability or veteran status, or any characteristic protected by law. Campus Safety Information Mason’s Annual Security and Fire Safety Report is available at http://police.gmu.edu/annual-security-report/ Apply Now! Sign up for job alerts! Job Alert 4a558746 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments College of Science College of Science Locations Fairfax, VA, Virginia, United States Fairfax, VA, Virginia, United States First Name (required) 1fac5b72 Last Name (required) 45f5bb7e Email (required) 79cf64dc Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Similar Jobs Instructional Technologist Division of Enrollment Management Join George Mason University as an Instructional Technologist and help advance engaging, innovative online learning through instructional design, educational technology, and faculty support. Graduate Admissions Specialist & Academic Advisor Antonin Scalia Law School The Graduate Admissions Specialist & Academic Advisor at George Mason University’s Antonin Scalia Law School has administrative support from various departments within the law school and the university. This position is primarily responsible for supporting student facing office functions including meeting with prospective students, overseeing front desk and staff support, managing office supplies and supporting application management, supporting onboarding functions for admitted students, and guiding current students through their program and university resources. Research Scientist Col of Engineering and Computing The Mason Flood Hazards Research Lab (Sid and Reva Dewberry Department of Civil, Environmental, and Infrastructure Engineering) is seeking a full time Research Scientist. Ready to Appl